        <div id="brightener" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Brightener Step Nobody Skips Twice
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you take one thing from this guide, take this: a caustic stripper is half a process, and using it without a brightener produces a worse-looking deck than you started with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium hydroxide leaves the wood surface strongly alkaline. Wood is naturally slightly acidic, and in that alkaline state it darkens, the grain lifts, and tannins in the timber mobilise and stain the surface grey and brown. People finish the stripping step, look at the result, and conclude they have ruined the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            A brightener — oxalic acid in most products — neutralises that alkalinity. The visual change is immediate and dramatic: the darkened surface lightens back toward fresh timber within minutes, the grey tannin staining lifts, and the wood looks like something you would want to stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also matters chemically rather than just cosmetically. Stain applied to an alkaline surface bonds poorly and fails early, and the raised grain left by stripping absorbs unevenly. Brightening restores a neutral, open surface that takes stain the way the manufacturer assumed it would.
          </p>

          <p>
            The full sequence is strip, rinse thoroughly, brighten, rinse thoroughly, dry for 48 hours, then stain. Every step exists because the previous one changed the wood, and shortening it is the single most common reason a restained deck disappoints.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Apply a Stripper Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Technique matters more here than product choice, and three details decide the result.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Let It Dry</h3>

          <p>
            A stripper works while it is wet. If it dries on the surface it stops working and becomes far harder to remove, often leaving a residue that shows through the new stain. Work in shade, on a cool day, in sections you can finish — and mist the surface with water if it starts to flash off.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Respect the Dwell Time</h3>

          <p>
            Most failures are impatience. The label dwell time — typically 15 to 45 minutes — is how long the chemistry needs to penetrate the film. Rinsing early lifts the top layer and leaves the rest, which produces the patchy result people blame on the product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rinse Far More Than Feels Necessary</h3>

          <p>
            Residual stripper left in the grain keeps working, keeps the surface alkaline, and interferes with the new stain. Rinse until the water runs clear, then rinse again. If you use a pressure washer, keep it under 1,500 psi with a wide fan tip — softwood decking carves easily, and the damage is permanent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Work Top Down</h3>

          <p>
            Rails and balusters first, deck boards last, so that run-off lands on surfaces you have not yet finished rather than on ones you have. It sounds obvious and it is the mistake most first-timers make.
          </p>
        </div>

