          <p>
            The bottom row is worth being honest about. A deck carrying four coats of solid stain may not be economically strippable — the labour and product cost can approach the cost of replacing the boards, and the result is rarely perfect. At that point, sanding, or accepting another coat of solid stain, are both reasonable decisions.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is also the argument for choosing semi-transparent over solid stain when you do restain. Semi-transparent finishes wear away rather than peeling, which means the next maintenance cycle is a clean and a recoat rather than a strip. Our <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/">stain type comparison</a> covers that trade-off in full.
          </p>
