        <div id="plants" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Protecting Plants, Surfaces and Yourself
          </h2>

          <p>
            Caustic strippers are genuinely hazardous chemicals, and the run-off goes somewhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wet everything down first.</strong> Soak surrounding planting, lawn and shrubs thoroughly with clean water before you start, and again afterwards. Wet foliage dilutes splashes and run-off; dry foliage absorbs them. Cover anything precious with plastic.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Protect adjacent surfaces.</strong> Sodium hydroxide etches glass, damages aluminium and stains masonry. Cover siding, windows, light fittings and any painted surfaces below the deck — run-off travels further than expected.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wear the right protection.</strong> Chemical-resistant gloves, not gardening gloves. Eye protection, because splashes from a brush come up. Long sleeves and closed shoes. These products cause chemical burns and the injuries happen on ordinary weekends to careful people.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mind the neighbours' water.</strong> Run-off that reaches a storm drain enters watercourses. Where possible, direct rinse water onto lawn or gravel where it can dilute and neutralise rather than into a drain.
          </p>
        </div>

          <p>
            A note on composite and hardwood decking, since both come up. Composite boards do not take stain and must never be stripped with a caustic product — it can permanently blotch the cap layer. Tropical hardwoods such as ipe and cumaru are dense enough that finishes sit largely on the surface, so they strip more easily than softwood but also grey faster afterwards, which means the window between brightening and staining is shorter rather than longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cedar and redwood deserve their own caution. Both are rich in tannins, and caustic strippers mobilise those tannins aggressively — a stripped cedar deck can come up startlingly dark. This is exactly the case where the brightener does the most work, and where skipping it produces the worst-looking result of any timber.
          </p>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. After Stripping: When to Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            The wood needs to be dry before it takes stain, and dry means drier than it looks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allow at least 48 hours of dry weather after the final rinse, and longer in humidity or cool conditions. A moisture meter is the honest test — under 15 percent is the usual target, and freshly rinsed decking can sit well above that for days even when the surface feels dry to the touch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check for raised grain before staining. Stripping lifts the fibres, and on a deck that will be walked on barefoot that matters. A light sanding with 60 to 80 grit on horizontal surfaces knocks it back without closing the pores that the brightener just opened.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then stain within a week or two rather than leaving it. Stripped, brightened wood is fully exposed — UV starts greying it again within days, and the surface you worked to create begins to degrade. Our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a> and <a href="/what-temperature-to-stain-a-deck/">temperature guide</a> cover the next step.
          </p>

          <p>
            One last note: do not test your new stain on the deck. Test it on an offcut or an inconspicuous board first. Stripped wood takes stain differently from the weathered surface you remember, and colours routinely come out darker than the sample card suggests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Cleaner Instead of a Stripper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sodium percarbonate products are excellent deck cleaners and will not remove a bonded finish. If the label says cleaner or brightener, it is not a stripper — and the two sit side by side on the same shelf in near-identical packaging.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Brightener</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A caustic stripper leaves the wood alkaline, dark and grain-raised. Without an acid brightener to neutralise it, the deck looks grey and blotchy and the new stain bonds poorly. This is half a process, not a product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Stripper Dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Strippers work while wet. Dried on the surface they stop working and leave a residue that shows through the new finish. Work in shade, in sections, and mist with water if it starts to flash off in the sun.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing Too Hard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Softwood decking carves at surprisingly low pressure, and the damage is permanent and very visible once stained. Stay under 1,500 psi with a wide fan tip, keep moving, and never use a zero-degree nozzle on a deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Before the Wood Is Dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stripped decking holds moisture for days after it feels dry to the touch. Stain applied over 15 percent moisture content will not penetrate properly and often fails within a season. Wait 48 hours minimum, and use a meter if you have one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best deck stain remover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Restore-A-Deck Stain Stripper is our overall pick — a powder concentrate you can mix strong for solid stain or weak for a tired semi-transparent, at a fraction of ready-to-use cost. For built-up solid stain or paint, Wolman DeckStrip is the heavier option. For railings and detail work, a solvent gel like Citristrip is the right tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to strip my deck before restaining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the existing finish has failed. Sprinkle water on it: if it beads, clean and recoat. If it soaks in but the finish is intact, clean and recoat. If it is peeling, flaking or cracking, it must come off, because anything applied over a failing finish will lift with it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I really need a wood brightener after stripping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Caustic strippers leave the wood alkaline, darkened and with the grain raised, which both looks bad and prevents the new stain bonding properly. An oxalic acid brightener neutralises the surface, restores the colour within minutes, and opens the pores so stain penetrates.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a deck cleaner remove stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Deck cleaners are usually sodium percarbonate, which removes dirt, mildew and grey weathered fibres but leaves a bonded finish intact. They are frequently bought by mistake because the packaging sits next to strippers and uses similar language. Look for sodium hydroxide if you need to strip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I strip solid stain from a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a heavy-duty caustic stripper, allow the full dwell time without letting it dry, and expect a second application if there are three or more coats. Solid stain forms a genuine film designed to resist weather, so lighter products often lift only the top layer and leave a patchwork.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will deck stripper kill my plants?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. Soak surrounding planting and lawn thoroughly with clean water before starting and again afterwards, and cover anything precious with plastic. Wet foliage dilutes run-off; dry foliage absorbs it. Direct rinse water onto lawn or gravel rather than into a storm drain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after stripping can I stain a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 48 hours of dry weather after the final rinse, and longer in humid or cool conditions. The target is under 15 percent moisture content, and freshly rinsed decking sits above that for days even when the surface feels dry. Then stain within a week or two, before UV starts greying the fresh wood.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

